   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Consider the curve (y^2 + 6y + 7)(1 - x^2) = 7, for which dy/dx = x(y^2 + 6y + 7)/((y + 3)(1 - x^2)). For a negative constant k, the horizontal line y = k is tangent to the curve. What is the value of k?"
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": -6
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A horizontal tangent needs the numerator of dy/dx to vanish: x = 0, or y^2 + 6y + 7 = 0, that is y = -3 + sqrt(2) or y = -3 - sqrt(2).",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "At y = -3 + sqrt(2) or y = -3 - sqrt(2) the factor y^2 + 6y + 7 is 0, so the left side of the curve equation is 0, not 7. No point of the curve has either y-value.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Subtract",
            -3,
            [
               "Sqrt",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Substitute x = 0: y^2 + 6y + 7 = 7, so y^2 + 6y = 0, which gives y = 0 or y = -6.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            6
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "At (0, -6) the denominator is (-3)(1) = -3, which is not zero, so the tangent there is horizontal. The negative value is k = -6.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Negate",
            6
         ]
      }
   ],
